  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/store.js[0m — localStorage load/save behind a versioned key ([38;5;153mtasks.v1[39m). Handles the cases that actually bite:
    absent key, malformed JSON, a value that isn't an array. Returns an empty list rather than throwing, so one bad
    write can't brick the page.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/reminders.js[0m — [1mthe keystone.[0m A pure function [38;5;153mcategorize(tasks, now)[39m returning [38;5;153m{overdue, dueToday, dueSoon, [39m
    [38;5;153mlater}[39m. No [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m inside, no DOM, no storage — [38;5;153mnow[39m is a parameter. All the time reasoning lives here and nowhere
    else.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/ui.js[0m — renders the reminder summary and the task list from state. Takes data, returns/patches DOM. No business
    logic.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/main.js[0m — wiring only: load, render, handle events, save, and a one-minute tick so a task can cross into overdue
    while you're looking at it.

  [1mData flow:[0m [38;5;153mstore.load()[39m → in-memory array → [38;5;153mcategorize(tasks, new Date())[39m → [38;5;153mui.render()[39m. Every mutation goes through
  the same path: mutate → save → re-categorize → re-render. One direction, no partial updates, no sync bugs.

  [1mWhy [38;5;153mreminders.js[39m is separate and pure:[0m date-boundary logic is where this feature will actually go wrong — midnight
  rollovers, "due today" when it's 11:58pm, timezone edges, a task due in the past being created. Injecting [38;5;153mnow[39m makes
  every one of those a plain unit test with no fake timers and no waiting.

  [1mTooling[0m — cheapest to set up now, before any code exists. My recommendation is the first two; e2e is hard to justify
  at this size.
